<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>Ultima Online 2 - UO2 Vault Log for 6/28/2000</B></font>
		<p></p>

<font face=arial size=-1><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> Let's begin :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> I'd like to thank everyone for coming tonight to the UO2 Vault's first Origin Dev Team chat
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> The topic tonight is Skill Trees
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> Tonight we have with us Ubiq, Windfeather, Usul, Mobius and Beast who are from the Dev Team
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> And Big_D, Drax and Ghosty will be fielding questions
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> To submit a question just type "/msg name question" where name is either Big_D, Drax or Ghosty and question is your question
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> Go ahead and start submitting your questions and we'll get going
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Alrighty...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Greetings and salutations all. 
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Katto* Will the entire skill tree be available to view no matter what level you are, or will you have to advance in certain areas to see what's "deeper" in the tree?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We're going to keep quite a bit of the skill tree system nice and open...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Meaning that you'll be able to see future skills that you will be able to get down the road...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> That way you can plan you choices for the entire career of your character...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> That isn't to say that we won't add a few mystery skills in from time to time that someone..
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> will just have to discover...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> In order to keep confusion down, we will only be showing the skills you can learn, or almost can learn inside the game.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Although I suspect they wouldn't remain a secret for too long. :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> However, we definitely will document most of the skill trees, as Mobius says, so you can plan your character's development in detail.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Showing you some 1000+ skills on day one is, shall we say, a little daunting. =)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Crunchalot* How does the Origin team plan to balance the various fighting types/magery in terms of pvp?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Balancing magic and phyiscal combat is always tricky...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We're hoping to allievate some of these problems by insuring that there's plenty...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> of skills in both magic and combat that will enable you to devote the entire life of your...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> character to just that one skill set...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> At higher levels of armed combat...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Character will have access to a number of special abilities such as powerful attack combinations...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> which essentially should be in the same realm of power in combat as spells...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> That way, mages don't get to have all of the fun... Fighters will have some really cool...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> special effects of their own.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Gorgrim_Ironfist* This is about the warrior skill tree, pure warrior's have always gotten the short end of the stick as compared to those of the magical influence. Will warrior's have some pretty interesting and fun skills to choose from at all?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Opps. This one was supposed to be me. Sorry. :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> As I was saying in my answer to the last question...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> keep on the ball!
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> At the higher levels pure warriors will have access to some very cool special abilities...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The most significant of these abilities are the attack combinations...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> The Mashed Potato...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> High level attack combinations will enable warriors to unleash a large string of attacks...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> In very rapid succession, that also generate other special effects...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Such as knocking down and stunning your opponent.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *drkfce* ok do you know what "level" the guys were supposed to be in the video, how long will it take to bet that kind of skill?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Hmmm, they were using very advanced weaponry, and performing some pretty wicked strokes.  They spent a lot of ankhs =)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> He's probably a master warrior. Using two weapons at one isn't easy. :)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Ar-Kane|SK* Can you name some school of magic and tell what skill they have?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> There are three tiers of magic.  The lower levels, the hybrid levels and the master levels.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> I'll talk more about it in the future, but the lower levels are the elemental schools from U8-9 (Fire, Earth, Air and Water).
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> And Beer.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> The hybrid schools are combinations of those elements, a study that the meer introduced.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Only serious mages will ever get the hybrid schools.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Stay tuned for more on the master schools.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Usul&gt;</b></font> think single malt scotch :)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *xavier* Any plans to include rare/hidden skills in the skill tree's that only show up given certain work in various previous skills? or will it be much more open-ended always moving up the line in sure ways?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Oh, there's very little linear about our skill trees.  We definitely have a pattern or structure that we use across all the skill trees
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> But they can grow and expand per skill tree, go in many different directions - and they definitely are NOT linear.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> And yes, there will be rare, hidden skills that are undocumented.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Ziffnab* Could you please tell us more about thief skills? Will they be simply stealing or will it be more in depth and interesting, like sneaking, looking at equip, and other recon like skills
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We've got a wide range of thieving activities...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We'll have stealing as well as hiding and stealth...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Hiding and stealth should prove to be exciting as the system will be more than simple hide if you...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> succeed and don't hide if you fail...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Whether or not you are detected will be based on an individual's perception...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Which is another thieving skill.  Perception will let you see hidden enemies...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'There's also poisioning, backstabbing, and lockpicking/trap disarm, to name a few.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> I'm personally a big fan of 'rogues', or adventuring thieves.  We've got some plans in place to make thieves very valuable to the discriminating party
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *The_Bicycler* Do you have any more info tidbit about "Fun" Skills such as, dancing, etc...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Only that we will have them!
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> See, even as much as I joke about it, skills like juggling can add a lot to the world
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> And roleplaying is such an important part of the experience that silly and fun things add to it, and some people will take that role
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> This is, of course, assuming that those skills are inexpensive.  But we're building social spaces, and fun and humor is an important part of that.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Moonboy* when you learn a new skill will it require more experience points to be raised or will it raise with usage?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We aren't using a use based system of any kind...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> So to get better in say, sword smithing, you need to buy more sword smithing skill boxes...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> with more skill points.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Moonboy* Will these powerful attack combinations require a series of button combinations to execute or will it be made more simple to execute?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> We have a pretty slick combat interface that will allow you to do your combo strokes with a single key press.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> It will most definitely not be a click fest...how you fight will be highly customizable :)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *g56AFK* Is it true that how you chose to deploy your skill points is NOT permenant. As in you can change all you points from swords to magery if you tire of swords? (this would be ALOT of fun in my opinion)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Yes, it's true.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Altough it will take time.  There's no transfering all your points over night.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> You can, on occasion, do a quest that will allow you to 'unlearn' one skill box, and throw those ankhs back into your pool
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> You can only do that quest so often though (currently once every two RT days) to prevent mages from becoming warriors overnight.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Darkmane* You guys said that skills are going to be binary right? So this means that every smith with, for example, basic sword crafting, will be equally good at it? On what failure/success will depend on?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> A basic crafting skill branch works sort of like this...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> They can, however, become bakers overnight.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'Let's say I pick up 'Sword Smithing'...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'That skill gives me the ability to craft a couple of different swords at a beginner's level...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The sword's won't be that good and I may fail some....
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> No sword of Whupass yet.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Now I have a choice to make...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> If I'm happing with just making shortswords that the 'Sword Smithing' gave me...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> I may elect to get better at making those shortswords...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'Therefore I pick up some skills that enable to craft shortswords more quickly and with less chance of failure...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> As I work down the branch, I'll also be able to make those shortswords be more durable...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> or do more damage, or be more accurate, etc...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Now if I want to craft more than just shortswords, I work down another section of the swordsmithing branch...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> I may pick up longsword smithing... after that vikingswords, then greatswords...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Reallygreatswords...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Therefore for the same amount of skill points I can essentially start to make some very nice...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> stuff.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> shortswords, or get down to making beginner quality greatswords...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Naturally if I want to make really good greatswords, I'm going to have to spend even more skill points...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'Whew that was a long one. :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> To us, the game UWOO is about character customization, and character customization isn't so much what your character looks like as what he can DO.  
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Smity* On the skill tree will Meer advance more quickly in magic than juka or human?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Racial bonuses affect skills in two ways...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> First off, certain skills are cheaper for certain races...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The cost bonus you get being a meer in magic may let you master fire magic a couple of levels sooner...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> than that of a Juka...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Different races also have the ability to learn skills that no other race may learn...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> For example, a human can learn 'Iolo's Deadly Crossbow Aiming'. It is essentially an extra damage...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> bonus to crossbows that neither meer or jukans can learn...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Good ol' Iolo.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Each race has sets of these unique skills.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Moonboy* How will the schools of magic differ? Will there only be different spell types or does each school have unique skills that are aquired along with it.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Juka's Jarring Jingoistic Jabbing.
<Vaxhacker> Incidentally, the comedy of Windfeather can be found on cassette and CD.  Check your local record store.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> SOme of the schools have similar spells (i.e. they all have ways to do damage)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> But fundamentally, it was really important to me that each spell school have their own strengths, weaknesses and identities.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> SOme schools have more stringent recharge times, or higher reagent costs.  And some schools have more flexibility than others.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *I* I don't know if you mentioned this earlier (I just joined) but what kind of trade skills will be included?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Basket weaving, bead stringing...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We've got lots of trade skills...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'To name a few...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Blacksmithing lets you make metal weapons, armor, shields, as well as a number of simple tools...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Cooking gives you access to a hundred or so different dishes as well as brewing...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Tinkering contains most of our technological items...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Tech weapons, tech armor, tech tools...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Tech flaming kittens.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> There's also tailoring, alchemy, fishing, mining... I'm probably forgetting a few.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Our goal is to make pretty much every item you could possibly want craftable.  The player driven economy is very important to us.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;BEAST_OSI&gt;</b></font> Lumberjacking and bow craft
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Yeah, those too.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> We think UO did an awesome job with player economy, and would like to continue that tradition.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Kamic* my question:How long would it take to max out a skill?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> There is no 'max out a skill' in UWOO.  However, at the culmination of the skill trees, you can become a 'GrandMaster'
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Where you are the culmination of that particular branch of the skill tree.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> NPCs swoon when you pass, throw flowers at your feet...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Many of the skill trees have multiple grandmasters in them - I think there are 4 or 5 potential grandmaster warriors you can be, each with different specializations and foci
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Needless to say, being a grandmaster is the pinnacle of study, and you should expect a lot of work to achieve that goal
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Vaxhacker&gt;</b></font> Kind of like a post-doc degree.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font>  *Doozer* Since there seem to be a whole heck of a lot of skills in the skill trees, I'm concerned about the usefulness of -individual- skills.  If they're super-specialized (door handle crafting!) it would just be annoying.  How "broad" are individual skills?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> I had the same concern myself when I arrived on the team...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Mobius had his entire wall plastered with skills.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Thousands of 'em.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> However, the way it works is:
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> All skills will either make something useful, or will be a modifier to make a useful thing more useful.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> (did he say thousands?  er, he meant hundreds.  yea)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> (Or be a useful spell, combat move, etc).  Well, it looked like thousands :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> It's not 100's of skills that all make something different, because you're correct, we'd have fluff.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Doozer* For craft skills, will it actually take time to make something?  I.e., more than 2 RL seconds per sword?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> A number of items can be made very quickly like in UO...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> The Bridge of Fish, however, takes weeks.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The high end stuff will take a good amount of time to make...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> A high end sword may take a couple of hours.  The Uber Sword of Death at the extreme level could...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> take days.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Ripley* Will you have to mix trade skills to make things, like mix tinkering with smithing to get the tech armor and weapons??
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> There will definitely be some hybrids going on
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> It's a way to encourage some specialization into some neat directions.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> We've even got a couple of weird ones like Lumberjacking and Fishing.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> *grin*
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The Lumberjack fisherman is powerful. :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> You chop down trees and hope they fall on the fish.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Gorgrim_Ironfist* Will the game be level based in advancement or will it be more like the original Uo in that there are no levels you just gain skill...?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Technically, UWOO is levelbased.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> In reality, though, you are the sum of your skills.  What level you are only roughly gauges how many skill points you have to spend.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> In fact, we've often discussed removing levels entirely from the design.  We've decided to leave it in, as new users find them a useful construct to measure their own progress.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Katto* Currently with UO, some things (mainly house add-ons) require ONE person to have all the skills necessary to make it.  In Origin, will it be possible to pass the unfinished product on to another crafter with the skill needed to finish it?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> There will be some of that, yes.  Forming symbiotic relationships between players for items of high value is necessary for an adventurer's economy to work.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Kamic* can players gain stats off other players?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> By this, I assume you are referring to how you can spar with each other in UO and learn...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Since our primary experience model will involve killing things, that would be a bit problematic.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> But I like sparring, so maybe we can work something out.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Drax* How would you make creating swords go over several days? Would there just be multiple parts of the sword to be crafted, or...?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> When you craft an item, it begins as a crafting project...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> As you work on the project, it moves closer and closer to completion...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> This concept allows you to stop and start the project over time...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> There's no need to complete it all at once...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Furthermore, crafting the uber item generally is a multistep process...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> You need to get your Whup and your Ass before you combine them, you see.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Crafting the base item is just the beginning as other crafting actions must be performed on it...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> in order to complete it.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> For example..
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> A tinker may want to be a really good clockwork longsword...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'First, he needs a blacksmith to craft him a really good longsword...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> 'That will take some time in and of itself...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> The tinker will want to use high end components in his sword, the best springs and gears and such...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> These pieces require their own specialist to get the very best quality...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Then, of course, the tinker needs to take all of these pieces and craft the complete clockwork longsword...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> After that, he may pass it off to a mage for further enhancement...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Each of these steps is a crafting project in and of itself.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Magius* what is the archery skills going to be like ? actual aiming? or auto aim?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> All of our combat will involve having a current target set, so when you do your attack, of whatever type, it will go to that target.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Actual aiming is problematic in a game that has to be lag tolerant.  You will autofire at a selected target
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Remember, people may not be even close to where you think they are =)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Durn LPB's.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Meric* Will we still have a tank mage problem, i.e., will it be possible to master all the skills in the game? Is time the only control for skill gain or are there other limits...for example can certain races only go so far along certain trees?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Definitely, definitely, definitely not.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> For sure, the man says.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> First off, in order to be a great warrior or a great wizard, you will have to devote the majority of your skill points to that discipline.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> Easy for him to say. I've got to balance it all. :)
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> In order to be a grandmaster, you will have to devote nearly all of the skill boxes you ever learn to those disciplines
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> If you try to mix and match something that requires an immense amount of learning (such as magic and combat), you will find that your abilities pale compared to those that devote their entire lives to the study.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> And JUST IN CASE the math defeats us, we are marking some skill boxes as mutually exclusive from each other.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Tank mages = lame.  No tank mages.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *G56* About racial bonuses : Will they be prominent differences or slight ones? I know that making a character and then finding out later that being a meer would of made me 20% more effective or even would of leveled 5% faster and been 3 levels higher I would feel cheated and couldnt do anything :(
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> We're being very careful not to create a situation where there is a 'best race' for a specific skill tree.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> Mobius told me he would kill me if I added a bonus that made all Juka the best smiths, for example.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> And he would kill me by taking me up in his plane and doing 6G stunts...so, no uber-racial sets.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> (By the way, I do very little work on skills)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Hondo* Here's something I haven't seen mentioned yet: How will a player's skills be visible to other players? This becomes important when you start looking for player-made goods.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> As you learn milestone skills...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> These skills grant you titles...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> By showing off these titles, people will get an idea of who you are...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Mobius&gt;</b></font> So you will know that Bob the Master Swordsmith is probably a good guy to get a longsword from.
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Volrath* is it possible to become a great char, while not fighting at all, rather by sitting in towns and crafting items of battle for people for profit?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Hmmmmmm.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> It is possible to build a great craftsman without fighting.  However, do not expect it to be easy, macroable, or without risk.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> So I guess 'no', since you can't just sit there. =)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> *Pwyll* Will there be skill boxes that need more than just ankhs and the prerequisite skill boxes to obtain? Perhaps a quest or some other requirement?
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> We're working on that one right now, actually...
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> While we don't have to have this for the system to work, it opens itself up to all sorts of possibilities, e.g. you must find a rare item to get that dragon to teach you the Flame Breath spell.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Windfeather&gt;</b></font> In fact, that would make a good question of the week :)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> I want to thank you all for coming to listen to us spout out about the skill trees.  I know that they seem a little wacky,
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> but the UWOO team feels like we really have found a solution for character advancement that really allows the level of character customization that players deserve and demand for building an online persona.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Words really cannot express how happy I am with the skill trees solution, how awesome they are in testing, and how many problems they solve almost effortlessly
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> The largest problem is balance, and we have had a QA staff testing the skill trees for the last year or so, 
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> trying to see if the balance is right.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> It's about time to bring this chat to a close, but you'll be hearing a lot more about the specifics of the skill trees in the very near future.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Feel free to keep asking questions about them on the message boards.  We're not claiming we're perfect, and we follow them closely.
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ubiq&gt;</b></font> Good night and thanks for coming. =)
<p><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> I'd like to thank the Dev Team for coming and participating tonight
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Ghosty&gt;</b></font> Me too!
<br><font color=000068><b>&lt;Gasper&gt;</b></font> And thanks to all of you for asking some really good questions and getting some really good answers out of the team :)
